<!--
  win_sysmon_health.xml  telemetry-health monitoring for SIEM01 (10.10.10.5)
  Author: Claude Code, at Johnny's direction (2026-10-13). Deployed to /var/ossec/etc/rules/. Registry range 100890-100899.

  Watches for Sysmon going dark the way it did on 2026-10-06, when the Sysmon64 service, driver and event channel
  disappeared from DC01 and SRV01 with no recorded cause and nothing alerted. The Service Control Manager writes these
  to the System channel, which every Windows agent already collects; fields confirmed from a retained event:
  win.eventdata.param1 is the service name ("Sysmon64"), param2 is the state ("running"/"stopped").

  100890 (7036 Sysmon64 stopped), level 10: opens a LabWatch incident. A normal reboot or shutdown also stops the
  service and will open an expected incident; that is the accepted cost of never missing a stop while the host stays up
  (the 2026-10-06 case). If reboot incidents become a nuisance, drop this to level 8 (alert only) or add a shutdown
  correlation. 100891 (7034 terminated unexpectedly), level 12: a crash or kill, never a clean shutdown.

  Honest limit: this is reactive. If Sysmon is already gone the agent still ships System-channel events, so a stop is
  caught, but a slow failure to emit event-1 data is not. A positive heartbeat (agent-side check that the service and
  channel exist) is the stronger control and is tracked as a separate follow-up.
-->
<group name="windows,system,sysmon_health,">

  <rule id="100890" level="10">
    <if_sid>61100</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^7036$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.param1" type="pcre2">(?i)^Sysmon(64)?$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.param2" type="pcre2">(?i)^stopped$</field>
    <description>Sysmon service ($(win.eventdata.param1)) entered the stopped state on $(win.system.computer): telemetry may be down (see the 2026-10-06 disappearance)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1562.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>attack,sysmon_health,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100891" level="12">
    <if_sid>61100</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^7034$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.param1" type="pcre2">(?i)^Sysmon(64)?$</field>
    <description>Sysmon service ($(win.eventdata.param1)) terminated unexpectedly on $(win.system.computer): not a clean shutdown</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1562.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>attack,sysmon_health,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
